    "summary": "Soaring Dark Star anchors strong first‑set jams in historic Cleveland show",
    "blurb": "The first set launches with a tight China > Rider and a fiery The Other One. Good Lovin' showcases a rare Phil bass solo, and the second‑set Dark Star soars with crisp guitar work. The audience tape is of fair quality but captures the key moments clearly.",
    "review": "On October 17, 1970 at Cleveland Music Hall the Dead delivered a performance that still shines despite being preserved only on an audience tape. The night opened powerfully with China Cat Sunflower segued into I Know You Rider, then burst into a fiery run through The Other One that set the tone for a first‑set full of tight jams. Good Lovin' in the second set featured a standout bass solo from Phil Lesh, and after a brief drum interlude the band launched into an extended Dark Star where Jerry Garcia’s soaring chords and ascending riff soar above the mix. Although the tape suffers from cuts (notably Truckin’ and Cold Rain & Snow) and occasional muffling, the highlight moments—especially Dark Star and Saint Stephen—come through with enough clarity to make this a worthwhile listen for completists and Deadheads interested in early‑70s improvisation.",
